In article <MEISSNER.94Jun5214725@pasta.osf.org>,\
 meissner@osf.org (Michael Meissner) writes...
> I just ascended a character and found it gets really annoying when you are
> wearing a ring of conflict in a populated room, such as the Astral plane (and
> the big room, barracks, etc.).  Rather than having to hit space after each and
> every message, it would be useful if the game switched to the More interface
> when there is more than one line.  That way you can read twenty messages at
> once on a terminal, and have a scrolling winding under X to handle the
> messages.  The same goes when you are frozen by a floating eye, and get
> multiple hits from monsters.

     The problem is worse on the astral level because so many monsters
there get mulitple attacks per turn and/or move fast enough to get extra
turns.  Also, their messages are often long enough to cause line wrap,
which aggravates the situation.  Better message handling would be nice
to have, but any scheme which overwrites the map (more or less essential
on an ordinary terminal due to lack of real estate...) will have to cope
with the fact that the player could lose some information (origin of
wand zaps and such, which are briefly visible as they occur).

     You can always hit <esc> at the --More-- prompt; then you won't
see any more messages until it's time to provide the next real input.
At that point, you can use <ctrl/P> to go back through the messages
which were skipped.  That approach does not work too well if the final
message is "game over" ;-) or if there are so many skipped that the
playback buffer can't hold them all.

     There's also the noVerbose option setting, but it has its own
drawbacks if you're used to the normal Verbose setting.



